<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec4-3" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Section 4.3</title>

  <definition xml:id="def-inverse">
    <title>Inverse Function</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For a function <m>f(x)</m>, which has domain <m>A</m> and range <m>B</m>, its <term>inverse function</term> is a function <m>\inv{f}(x)</m> which has the property that 
        <md>
          <mrow> f(\inv{f}(x)) = x</mrow>
        </md>
        and
        <md>
          <mrow> \inv{f}(f(x)) = x \amp </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        The domain of the inverse function <m>\inv{f}(x)</m> is the range of the function <m>f(x)</m>, and the range of the inverse function <m>\inv{f}(x)</m> is the domain of the function <m>f(x)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  
  <example xml:id="sec4-3-inverse-ex1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        For the function <m>f(x) = 8x + 1</m>, we can find its inverse by doing the following procedure.
      </p>
      <p>
        First, we switch the places of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>:
        <md>
          <mrow> y \amp = 8x + 1 </mrow>
          <mrow> x \amp = 8y + 1 </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Next, we solve for <m>y</m>.
        <md>
          <mrow> x \amp = 8y + 1 </mrow>
          <mrow> x -1  \amp = 8y </mrow>
          <mrow>\dfrac{1}{8}(x-1) \amp = y  </mrow>
        </md>
        so that our final answer is
        <md>
          <mrow>y = \dfrac{x-1}{8} </mrow>
        </md>
        or
        <md>
          \inv{f}(x) = \dfrac{1}{8}x - \dfrac{1}{8}
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        We can verify that these are inverse functions by composing. If <m>f(x) = 8x + 1</m> and <m>\inv{f}(x) = \dfrac{1}{8}x - \dfrac{1}{8}</m>, then
        <md>
          <mrow> (f\circ \inv{f})(x) \amp = f(\inv{f}(x)) </mrow>
          <mrow> \amp = f\lrpar{\dfrac{1}{8}x - \dfrac{1}{8}} </mrow>
          <mrow> \amp = 8\lrpar{\dfrac{1}{8}x - \dfrac{1}{8}} + 1 </mrow>
          <mrow> \amp = x - 1 + 1 </mrow>
          <mrow> \amp = x </mrow>
        </md>
        which tells us that the two functions are inverses.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>
</section>